<article class="doc">
<h1 class="page">Errors</h1>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="Introduction"><a class="anchor" href="#Introduction"></a>Introduction</h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>This API follows the Remote Procedure Call (RPC) programming pattern. To understand the root cause and the solution to a problem, it&#8217;s important to identify the layer to which the error belongs.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="Transport_Layer"><a class="anchor" href="#Transport_Layer"></a>Transport Layer</h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Transport oriented errors are returned as HTTP statuses and can be accompanied by more details in the response to the JSON message.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="_error_object"><a class="anchor" href="#_error_object"></a>Error Object</h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>Error</code> object contains the following parameters:</p>
</div>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>status</code>: The status of the error, indicating whether the request was processed or not.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>type</code>: An application-specific error type, or one from the list of typical Sabre API errors.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>errorCode</code>: A specific code representing the error.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>timeStamp</code>: Provides the exact point in time, expressed in UTC, when the response was generated.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>message</code>: A human-readable description of what went wrong.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
